<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-1-known-integrals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>A Selected Review of MTH 132</title>
  <subsection xml:id="ssec-summation-notation">
    <title></title>
    <p>
      Often times in this course, and especially during the <xref ref="ch-4-sequences-series">Sequences and Series</xref> chapter, 
      it will be useful to use summation notation.
      Generally, it looks like the following:
      <md>
        \sum_{k=1}^n f(k)
      </md>

      where <m>f</m> is a function or formula that is used to define each term, and <m>k</m> is an <em>index</em> that tells us what terms we are going to add up.
      The index runs through all integers from the lower bound to the upper bound in the summation (here the lower index is <m>1</m> and the upper index is <m>n</m>).

      So, the summation above represents the sum of the terms 
      <md>f(1) + f(2) + \cdots + f(n).</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, say we want to find a compact way to represent the sum of the first <m>10</m> squares:
      <md>
        1^2 + 2^2 + 3^2 + \cdots + 10^2.
      </md>
      Each term of this sum looks like <m>k^2</m> for some integer <m>k</m>, so that tells us that our formula for each term is <m>f(k) = k^2</m>. To represent every term in the sum, we need to use all integers between <m>1</m> and <m>10</m> inclusive. This tells us that 
      our index <m>k</m> (the variable used in the formula) will run from <m>1</m> to <m>10</m>, and hence we can represent the above some in summation notation as
      <md>
        \sum_{k=1}^{10} k^2.
      </md>
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write out and compute <md>\sum_{k=1}^{5} k(k+1).</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We can explicitly write out the sum by substituting each integer from 1 to 5 into the formula <m>k(k+1)</m> and adding them together. This gives
          <md>
            1(1+1) + 2(2+1) + 3(3+1) + 4(4+1) + 5(5+1) = 1\cdot2 + 2\cdot3 + 3\cdot4 + 4\cdot5 + 5\cdot6 = 2 + 6 + 12 + 20 + 30 = 70.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Convert the following sum into summation notation:
          <md>
            2+4+6+8+\dots+2n.
          </md>
          
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Every term in this summation is an even number, which means it can be written as <m>2 \cdot k</m> for some integer <m>k</m> (just like the last term shown), so our formula will be <m>f(k)=2k</m>. 
          To get the smallest number in the sum, <m>2</m>, we need to start at <m>k=1</m>, and to get the largest number in the sum, <m>2n</m>, we need to end at <m>k=n</m>.
          Hence the range on our index <m>k</m> is from <m>1</m> to <m>n</m>, and hence 
          <md>
            \sum_{k=1}^{n} 2k.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Convert the following sum into summation notation:
          <md>
            3x + \frac{3}{4}x^2+\frac{3}{16}x^3+\frac{3}{64}x^4
          </md>        
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          There are a few ways to do this, but ultimately we need to find a pattern or formula in the terms. The two clearest patterns are that each term has a <m>3</m> out front
          and each term has <m>x^k</m> for some exponent <m>k</m>. Since this exponent <m>k</m> ranges from <m>1</m> to <m>4</m>, we will use <m>k</m> as our index, and the range will
          be from <m>1</m> to <m>4</m>. The last thing we have to do is find a pattern for the denominators, which are <m>1,4,16,64</m>. Notice that we can get from one denominator to the next 
          by multiplying by <m>4</m>, so each term is <m>4^m</m> for some integer <m>m</m>. Notice that <m>m</m> and <m>k</m> are not the same, since if we plugged <m>k=1</m> into <m>\frac{3}{4^k}x^k</m>, 
          we would get <m>\frac{3}{4}x</m>, which is not the first term. So when I plug in <m>k=1</m>, I want <m>1=4^0</m>, for <m>k=2</m> I want <m>4=4^1</m>, an so on. Since <m>k</m> is always exactly <m>1</m>
          larger than the exponent we need in the denominator, we can use the formula <m>4^{k-1}</m> for the denominators. Hence, we can represent the sum as
          <md>
            \sum_{k=1}^{4} \frac{3}{4^{k-1}} x^k.
          </md>

          Note: There are other equivalent ways of writing this sum through <em>reindexing</em>, which is discussed below.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p><alert>Reindexing</alert></p>
    <p>
      While summation notation can be helpful, the same sum can often be represented by different formulas or using different indicies. For instance, the following sums
      <md>
        \sum_{k=1}^{5} k \quad \text{and} \quad \sum_{j=0}^{4} (j+1)
      </md>
      both represent the same sum, namely <m>1+2+3+4+5</m>. In this case the indicies <m>k</m> and <m>j</m> are related via the formula <m>k=j+1</m>. Once you have such a formula, 
      one can determine how the lower bounds and upper bounds change via substituting the lowest and highest values of the new index into the relationship.
    </p>

    <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Reindex the following sums so that the indicies run from <m>1</m> to <m>4</m>:
            <ol>
              <li><p><md>\sum_{k=0}^{3} (k+1)</md></p></li>
              <li><p><md>\sum_{k=2}^{5} \frac{3}{4^{k-2}}x^{k-1}</md></p></li>
            </ol>
            
          </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ssec-review-of-integration">
    <title>Review of Integration</title>

    <p> The definite integral gives the cumulative total of many small
    parts, such as the slivers which add up to the area under a graph. Numerically, it is a 
    limit of Riemann sums 
    <md> \int_a^b f(x) dx \ = \lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i)\ \Delta x</md>
    where we divide the interval <m> x \in [a,b] </m> into <m>n</m> increments
    of size <m>\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{n}</m> with division points <m>a &lt; a + \Delta x &lt; a + 2\Delta x &lt; \cdots &lt; a + n\Delta x = b</m>, 
    and <m>x_1,\ldots x_n</m> are sample points from each increment.  This definition is not a theoretical curiosity: it is the reason integrals
    are relevant to physical problems, and it is the only way to evaluate most integrals: there is no algebraic way.
    </p>

    <p>However, for sufficiently simple functions <m>f(x)</m>, we can evaluate integrals algebraically by the shortcut of the Second 
      Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. This says that if <m>f(x)</m> is the rate of change of some known antiderivative <m>F(x)</m>, 
      then the integral of <m>f(x)</m> is the cumulative total change of <m>F(x)</m>:
      <md> F'(x)=f(x) \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad \int_a^b f(x)\,dx \ =\ \left.F(x)\right|_{x=a}^{x=b}\ =\ F(b)-F(a)\,.</md>

      (The First Fundamental Theorem says that the definite integral gives an antiderivative even if there is no formula <m>F(x)</m>:
      defining <m>I(x)= \int_a^x f(t)\,dt</m>, we have <m>I'(x)  = f(x)</m>.)
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ssec-fundamental-integrals">
    <title>Fundamental Integrals</title>
    
    <p>
      Algebraic integration is the process of finding antiderivative formulas, denoted as indefinite integrals <m>\int\! f(x)\,dx=F(x)+C</m>. 
      The most direct method is to reverse Basic Derivatives, such as <m>(x^p)' = px^{p-1}</m> reversing to <md>\int\! x^p\,dx =\frac{x^{p+1}}{p+1}.</md> 
      We can do the same for other familiar functions, as shown below:
    </p>
      
    <tabular>
      <col> </col>
      <col> </col>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell>Differentiation Rule</cell>
        <cell>Integration Rule</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md>\frac{d}{dx}(\sin x) = \cos x</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int \cos x \, dx = \sin x</md></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md> \frac{d}{dx}(\cos x) = -\sin x</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int \sin x \, dx = -\cos x</md></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md> \frac{d}{dx}(\tan x) = \sec^2 x</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int \sec^2 x \, dx = \tan x</md></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md> \frac{d}{dx}(e^x) = e^x</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int e^x \, dx = e^x</md></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md> \frac{d}{dx}(\ln x) = \frac{1}{x}</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int \frac{1}{x} \, dx = \ln |x|.</md></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <p>
    We also used implicit differentiation to find derivatives of inverse functions, which gave us the following results:
    </p>

    <tabular>
      <row>
        <cell>Differentiation Rule</cell>
        <cell>Integration Rule</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md>\frac{d}{dx} \arcsin x = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}} dx = \arcsin x</md></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md>\frac{d}{dx} \arccos x = -\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int -\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}} dx = \arccos x</md></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md>\frac{d}{dx} \arctan x = \frac{1}{1+x^2}</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int \frac{1}{1+x^2} dx = \arctan x</md></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md>\frac{d}{dx} \sec^{-1} x = \frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int \frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}} dx = \sec^{-1} x</md></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md>\frac{d}{dx} \csc^{-1} x = -\frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int \frac{-1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}} dx = \csc^{-1} x</md></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><md>\frac{d}{dx} \cot^{-1} x = -\frac{1}{1+x^2}</md></cell>
        <cell><md>\int -\frac{1}{1+x^2} dx = \cot^{-1} x</md></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <p>
    Our only other integration method so far is the Substitution Method, which reverses the Chain Rule:
    <md> \int\! f(g(x))\,g'(x)\,dx \ =\ \int \! f(u)\,du\ =\ F(u) +C\ =\ F(g(x)) + C, </md> 
    where <m>u=g(x)</m>, <m>du=g'(x)\,dx</m>, and <m>F'(u)=f(u)</m>.

    Using the substitution method, and some of the known integrals above, we can also derive the following:
    
    <md>
      \int \tan x \, dx = \ln |\sec x| + C \qquad \text{ and } \qquad \int \cot x \, dx = \ln |\csc x| + C.
    </md>
    
    We can conclude that since 
    <md>
      <mrow> \int \tan x \, dx \amp = \int \frac{\sin x}{\cos x} \, dx \qquad u= \cos x,\, du = -\sin x \,dx </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = -\int \frac{1}{u} du = -\ln |u|</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = -\ln |\cos x| = \ln |\cos x|^{-1} = \ln |\sec x| + C </mrow>
    </md>

    and similarly

    <md>
      <mrow> \int \cot x \, dx \amp = \int \frac{\cos x}{\sin x} \, dx \qquad u= \sin x,\, du = \cos x \,dx </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \int \frac{1}{u} du = \ln |u|</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp= \ln |\sin x| = \ln |\sin x|^{-1} = \ln |\csc x|. </mrow>
    </md>
  </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="ssec-linear-scaling">
    <title>Linear Scaling</title>
    <p>
      While this is a consequence of the Substitution Method, it is worth explicitly showing how the integral of
      functions like <m>e^{3x}</m> or <m>\sin(5x)</m> are related to the integrals of <m>e^x</m> and <m>\sin x</m> respectively.

      Starting with the exponential example, recall that by the chain rule, for any constant <m>a</m>
      <md>
        \frac{d}{dx} e^{ax} = a e^{ax}.
      </md>
      So for any <m>a \neq 0</m>, we have <m>\frac{d}{dx} \frac{1}{a} e^{ax} = e^{ax}</m>. By undoing the differentiation to get an antiderivative formula, we have
      <md>
        \int e^{ax} \, dx = \frac{1}{a} e^{ax} + C
      </md>
      for any constant <m>a \neq 0</m>. Similarly, for any constant <m>a</m>, we have
      <md>
        \frac{d}{dx} \sin(ax) = a \cos(ax).
      </md>
      Hence, in a similar way, we find out that for any <m>a \neq 0</m>
      <md>
        \int \cos(ax) \, dx = \frac{1}{a} \sin(ax) + C.
      </md>
      
      To see one more example, and to make things slightly more general, notice that by the chain rule, for any constants <m>a,b</m>, we have that 
      <md>
        \frac{d}{dx} \tan(ax+b) = a\sec^2(ax+b).
      </md>
      When <m>a \neq 0</m>, we can rearrange to say <m>\frac{d}{dx} \frac{1}{a} \tan(ax+b) = \sec^2(ax+b)</m>. Hence, we can conclude that for any constants 
      <m>a,b</m> with <m>a \neq 0</m>, we have
      <md>
        \int \sec^2(ax+b) \, dx = \frac{1}{a} \tan(ax+b) + C.
      </md>

      The purpose of these examples is to illustrate that if we know the antiderivative of a function <m>f(x)</m> is <m>F(x)</m>, then the
      antiderivative of <m>f(ax+b)</m> is <m>\frac{1}{a} F(ax+b)</m> for any constants <m>a,b</m> with <m>a \neq 0</m>. We can see this rigorously by the substitution rule, since
      
      <md> 
        <mrow> \int f(ax+b) \, dx = \int f(u) \frac{du}{a} \qquad \text{where } u=ax+b, \, du = a dx </mrow>
        <mrow> = \frac{1}{a} \int f(u) du = \frac{1}{a} F(u) + C = \frac{1}{a} F(ax+b) + C. </mrow>
      </md>

      This means that we can generalize every integral we have learned so far to include a linear scaling of the variable <m>x</m> in the integrand.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rmk-linear-scaling">
      <title>A Linear Scaling Warning</title>
      
      <p>
        This method only works for linear scalings. For example, 
        <md>
          \frac{d}{dx} e^{x^2} = 2x e^{x^2}, 
        </md>
        but
        <md>\int e^{x^2} \, dx \neq \frac{1}{2x} e^{x^2} + C.</md>

        Indeed, 
        <md>
          \frac{d}{dx} \left(\frac{1}{2x} e^{x^2}\right) =  e^{x^2} - \frac{1}{2x^2} e^{x^2} \neq e^{x^2}.
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ssec-ch-0-examples">
    <title>Examples</title>
    <p>
      With all of these techniques, we can now evaluate a good number of integrals, especially when we combine these techniques
      with other algebraic techniques. 
    </p>
    <example xml:id="ex-exp-1">
      <p> 
        Evaluate <md>\int (3e^2x)^3 \, dx.</md>.
      </p>
      <solution>
        <p> 
          As it stands, the integral is not in a form that we have discussed so far. However, we can use exponent rules to
          help us rewrite the integrand as
          <md>
            (3e^2x)^3 = 3^3 \cdot e^{2x \cdot 3} = 27 e^{6x}.
          </md>
          Now we can use linear scaling to evaluate the integral:

          <md>
            <mrow>\int (3e^2x)^3 \, dx = \int 27 e^{6x} \, dx = \amp 27 \int e^{6x} \, dx </mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = 27 \cdot \frac{1}{6} e^{6x} + C = \frac{9}{2} e^{6x} + C.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
      </example>

      <example xml:id="ex-exp-2">
        <p> 
          Evaluate <md>\int \frac{1}{e^{2x-1}} \, dx.</md>
        </p>
        <solution>
          <p> 
            Similar to <xref ref="ex-exp-1"/>, we can use exponent rules to rewrite the integrand as
            <md>
              \frac{1}{e^{2x-1}} = e^{-(2x-1)} = e^{-2x+1}.
            </md>
            Now we can use linear scaling to evaluate the integral:
            <md>
              \int \frac{1}{e^{2x-1}} \, dx = \int e^{-2x+1} \, dx = \frac{e^{-2x+1}}{-2} + C = -\frac{1}{2} e^{-2x+1} + C.
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>
      
      <example xml:id = "ex-exp-3">
        <p>
          Evaluate <md>(e^x + e^{-x})^2.</md>
        </p>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Similar to <xref ref="ex-exp-1"/> and <xref ref="ex-exp-2"/>, the integrand is not in a form that we have discussed, so
            we hope to use algebraic techniques to rewrite the integrand. In this case, we can use the expand and distribute the inside to find that
            <md>
              (e^x+e^{-x})^2 = (e^x)^2 + 2e^x e^{-x} + (e^{-x})^2 = e^{2x} + 2 + e^{-2x}.
            </md>
            Now that we can integrate each term individually, we can evaluate the integral:
            <md>
              <mrow>\int (e^x + e^{-x})^2 \, dx \amp = \int e^{2x} \, dx + \int 2 \, dx + \int e^{-2x} \, dx </mrow>
              <mrow>\amp = \frac{1}{2} e^{2x} + 2x - \frac{1}{2} e^{-2x} + C.</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <example xml:id="ex-exp-4">
        <p>
          Evaluate <md>\int \sqrt{e^x} \, dx.</md>
        </p>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Here, it is important to recall that the square root of a number is the same as raising that number to the one-half power. 
            So we can rewrite the integrand as
            <md>
              \sqrt{e^x} = (e^x)^{1/2} = e^{x/2}.
            </md>
            Now we can use linear scaling to evaluate the integral:
            <md>
              \int \sqrt{e^x} \, dx = \int e^{x/2} \, dx = \frac{e^{x/2}}{1/2} + C = 2 e^{x/2} + C.
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <example xml:id="ex-substitution">
      <p>
        Evaluate <md>\int x(x-1)^{100} \, dx.</md>
      </p>
      <solution>
        <p>
          In principle, we could approach this problem by expanding the integrand and integrating each term individually. 
          However, this would be a very tedious process, and even doing it as efficiently as possible with the binomial theorem would 
          result in an expression with 101 terms! 
          
          Instead, we can use the substitution method to evaluate the integral. The difficultly we have is that we would prefer to have
          one term raised to the hundredth power, which would allow us to apply our known rules. We can do that by calling that inside bit
          <m>u</m> and rewriting everything from there. So let <m>u = x-1</m>, which makes <m>du = dx</m>. Since we need to write everything from the 
          original integral in terms of the new variable <m>u</m>, it is helpful to notice that since <m>u = x-1</m>, we have <m>x = u+1</m>. 
          Then, by substituting these new expressions into the original integral and expanding, we have
          <md>
            <mrow> \int x(x-1)^{100} \, dx \amp = \int (u+1)u^{100} \, du = \int u^{101} + u^{100} \, du </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{102} u^{102} + \frac{1}{101} u^{101} + C. </mrow>
          </md>
          But, our answer is not complete until we substitute back in the original variable <m>x</m> to get
          <md>
            \int x(x-1)^{100} \, dx = \frac{1}{102} (x-1)^{102} + \frac{1}{101} (x-1)^{101} + C.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-sub-2">
      <p>
        Evaluate <md>\int \frac{5e^{2x}}{1+e^{4x}} \, dx.</md>
      </p>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Similar to our stategy in <xref ref="ex-substitution"/>, we can use a substitution to rewrite the integrand in a 
          more manageable form. In this case, we can use the substitution <m>u = e^{2x}</m> (this makes the denominator <m>1+u^2</m>, and
          the numerator looks like <m>du</m>) and hence <m>du = 2e^{2x} dx</m> and so <m>\frac{du}{2} = e^{2x} dx</m>.
          Then, we can rewrite the integral as
          <md>
            <mrow>\int \frac{5e^{2x}}{1+e^{4x}} \, dx \amp = \int \frac{5}{1+u^2} \cdot \frac{du}{2} </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{5}{2} \int \frac{1}{1+u^2} \, du = \frac{5}{2} \arctan u + C </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{5}{2} \arctan(e^{2x}) + C.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>

    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>